From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 15:15:12 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 144/145] feat(refinery): reject mode returns a MISS to the
 pool before rebase and trims the batch (gcp-s7j4.4)

acceptance_check = reject was wired as warn. It now does what warn does and
then takes every bead the refinery no longer holds out of the iteration, so a
bead whose acceptance read is MISS goes back to the polecat pool before rebase,
as a rebase conflict sends it back, and the rest of its batch still merges.

The fence takes exactly three edits: reject joins warn in one arm, the guard
becomes "not off", and the SKIP record carries the mode. A new
acceptance-reject block runs once after the last record. It does nothing unless
the var renders to reject; otherwise it reads each member's assignee, prints a
LEFT line for every bead no longer assigned to the refinery, cuts those members
from the manifest (the rule is "no longer assigned to me", not "MISS", because
that is the fact record's pool return produces), and ends with BATCH, INFO or
HEAD LEFT. stack already reads only .members[].id when .head matches, so it takes
the trimmed list unchanged and merge-batch.sh is untouched. When the head left,
the step pours the next iteration and burns this one with a byte-identical copy
of the rebase conflict path's block, the fifth pour that continues the
conversation.

The var description, the step intro, the prose after the fence and the
patrol-summary bullet say what reject does. The rubric is unchanged, because it
was measured.

Validation: test_refinery_acceptance_wiring.sh passes all 16 cases and
test_refinery_restart_cadence.sh passes with five stamp blocks; both were red
before this change (1a18258a).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
