-Warn mode never blocks. Whatever the verdicts, close this step and proceed to rebase. A `record` that exits 1 or 2
-is a WARN: name it in patrol-summary and proceed. With no `READ` line, close this step.
+A `record` that exits 1 or 2 is a WARN: name it in patrol-summary and proceed. With no `READ` line, close this
+step and proceed to rebase.
+
+After the last `record`, run this block ONCE, in one shell, with `$WORK` set as above. Outside reject mode it
+prints nothing and changes nothing. In reject mode `record` has already returned each MISS to the polecat pool,
+and this block takes every bead no longer assigned to you out of this iteration:
+```bash
+# --- acceptance-reject:begin ---
+if [ "{{acceptance_check}}" = reject ]; then
+  REJ_MANIFEST="$(git rev-parse --git-dir)/refinery-batch.json"
+  REJ_IDS="$WORK"
+  if [ -f "$REJ_MANIFEST" ]; then
+    REJ_MEMBERS=$(jq -r '.members | if type == "array" then .[].id else empty end' "$REJ_MANIFEST" 2>/dev/null)
+    [ -n "$REJ_MEMBERS" ] && REJ_IDS="$REJ_MEMBERS"
+  fi
+  REJ_GONE=""
+  REJ_HEAD_GONE=""
+  for REJ_ID in $REJ_IDS; do
+    REJ_JSON=$(gc bd show "$REJ_ID" --json 2>/dev/null)
+    if [ $? -ne 0 ] || [ -z "$(printf '%s' "$REJ_JSON" | jq -r '.[0].id // empty' 2>/dev/null)" ]; then
+      echo "WARN: could not read $REJ_ID; it stays in this iteration."
+      continue
+    fi
+    REJ_ASSIGNEE=$(printf '%s' "$REJ_JSON" | jq -r '.[0].assignee // empty' 2>/dev/null)
+    if [ "$REJ_ASSIGNEE" != "$GC_AGENT" ]; then
+      echo "LEFT: $REJ_ID (assignee now '$REJ_ASSIGNEE')"
+      REJ_GONE="${REJ_GONE:+$REJ_GONE }$REJ_ID"
+      [ "$REJ_ID" = "$WORK" ] && REJ_HEAD_GONE=1
+    fi
+  done
+  if [ -n "$REJ_HEAD_GONE" ]; then
+    rm -f "${REJ_MANIFEST:?}"
+    echo "HEAD LEFT: $WORK"
+  elif [ -n "$REJ_GONE" ]; then
+    REJ_KEPT=""
+    REJ_TMP="${REJ_MANIFEST}.tmp"
+    if jq --arg gone "$REJ_GONE" '.members |= map(select(.id as $i | ($gone | split(" ") | index($i)) | not))' "$REJ_MANIFEST" >"$REJ_TMP" 2>/dev/null &&
+       mv -f "$REJ_TMP" "$REJ_MANIFEST"; then
+      REJ_KEPT=$(jq -r '.members[].id' "$REJ_MANIFEST" 2>/dev/null | paste -sd ' ' -)
+    fi
+    rm -f "${REJ_TMP:?}"
+    if [ "$(printf '%s\n' "$REJ_KEPT" | wc -w | tr -d ' ')" -ge 2 ]; then
+      echo "BATCH: $REJ_KEPT"
+    else
+      rm -f "${REJ_MANIFEST:?}"
+      echo "INFO: batch of 1."
+    fi
+  fi
+fi
+# --- acceptance-reject:end ---
+```
+If it printed `HEAD LEFT: <id>`, this iteration has no head. Pour the next iteration and burn this one, exactly as
+a rebase conflict does, then close this step:
+```bash
+CURRENT_WISP=${GC_BEAD_ID:-}
+if [ -z "$CURRENT_WISP" ]; then
+  CURRENT_WISP=$(gc bd list --assignee="$GC_AGENT" --status=open,in_progress --type=molecule --include-infra --limit=0 --json | jq -r '.[0].id // empty')
+fi
+if [ -z "$CURRENT_WISP" ]; then
+  echo "ERROR: could not resolve the current refinery wisp: GC_BEAD_ID is unset and no open/in_progress patrol wisp is assigned to $GC_AGENT."
+  echo "ERROR: refusing to continue. With CURRENT_WISP empty this cycle would pour a successor and never burn the wisp it is running, leaking one wisp every cycle, while every command still exits 0."
+  gc runtime drain-ack
+  exit 1
+fi
+NEXT=$(gc bd mol wisp mol-refinery-patrol --root-only --var target_branch={{target_branch}} --var rig_name={{rig_name}} --var binding_prefix={{binding_prefix}} --json | jq -r '.new_epic_id // empty')
+if [ -z "$NEXT" ]; then
+  echo "Could not pour next refinery wisp; not burning."
+  gc runtime drain-ack
+  exit 1
+fi
+# --- session-iteration-stamp:begin ---
+# Carry the conversation's iteration count onto NEXT in the same write that
+# assigns it, so check-inbox can restart at restart_after_iterations. The count
+# continues only while CURRENT was stamped under this GC_CONTINUATION_EPOCH;
+# any other epoch was an earlier conversation, so NEXT starts again at 1. With
+# the epoch unset nothing is stamped and the cadence check reads "off".
+set -- --assignee="$GC_AGENT"
+if [ -n "${GC_CONTINUATION_EPOCH:-}" ]; then
+  if ! STAMP_JSON=$(gc bd show "$CURRENT_WISP" --json 2>/dev/null); then
+    echo "WARN: could not read $CURRENT_WISP; the next wisp's iteration count restarts at 1."
+    STAMP_JSON=""
+  fi
+  STAMP_EPOCH=$(printf '%s' "$STAMP_JSON" | jq -r '.[0].metadata.session_epoch // empty' 2>/dev/null)
+  STAMP_K=$(printf '%s' "$STAMP_JSON" | jq -r '.[0].metadata.session_iterations // empty' 2>/dev/null)
+  case "$STAMP_K" in ''|*[!0-9]*) STAMP_K=0 ;; esac
+  if [ "$STAMP_EPOCH" = "$GC_CONTINUATION_EPOCH" ]; then
+    STAMP_K=$((STAMP_K + 1))
+  else
+    STAMP_K=1
+  fi
+  set -- "$@" --set-metadata session_epoch="$GC_CONTINUATION_EPOCH" --set-metadata session_iterations="$STAMP_K"
+fi
+if ! gc bd update "$NEXT" "$@"; then
+  echo "Could not assign next refinery wisp; not burning."
+  gc runtime drain-ack
+  exit 1
+fi
+# --- session-iteration-stamp:end ---
+if [ -n "$CURRENT_WISP" ]; then
+  gc bd mol burn "$CURRENT_WISP" --force
+else
+  echo "ERROR: could not resolve the current refinery wisp; not burning. The patrol loop cannot advance — this wisp leaks and no successor runs."
+  gc runtime drain-ack
+  exit 1
+fi
+```
+Otherwise close this step and proceed to rebase, whatever the verdicts. `stack` takes the batch as the block
+left it.
 
